{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And anyone who looked at the serpent of copper lived.</b> The opposite of nature, for one who is bitten is endangered by looking at the form of the biter, and Moses made it of copper, which is reddish, resembling the seraph serpents. And so Hezekiah was healed by the application of a fig-cake to the boil, and the bitter waters were sweetened by that same tree which was bitter. And so this was a miracle within a miracle. So too here, that they were healed by looking at the form of the serpent, was a miracle within a miracle, for it is known from the science of nature regarding those bitten by a rabid dog that if they look at water, the likeness of the dog or of the injurer appears to them in it and they die; and so the medical scholars mentioned this in their books, and our Rabbis of blessed memory mentioned this in tractate Yoma, chapter Yom ha-Kippurim: anyone bitten by a dog, for all twelve months of the year, does not drink water except from a snake-trough, lest he see the dog and be endangered. And so the physicians warn against mentioning the name of the biter in the presence of the bitten, for their soul cleaves to that thought and does not separate from it, to the point that even in their urine they see the likeness of whelps. And since this matter is true and established among the wonders of the science of nature, it would have been proper for Israel, those bitten by the seraph serpents, not to see a serpent and not to mention it, for even the memory harms — how much more so the sight! Therefore this matter was a miracle within a miracle: to remove the harm from them by means of the injurer, and to heal the illness by means of that which causes illness. And the Holy One, blessed be He, commanded Moses to make the likeness of the seraph that killed them, and then they would be healed — to make known that it is not the serpent that kills, but the sin, as they said: it is not the serpent that kills, but the sin."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:36.004099836Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}